# Reflection: Cloud Computing in Daily Life and Version Control

## 1. Cloud Services I Use Regularly
It is incredible to realize how much our routine depends on cloud computing. From listening to music while studying to watching shows or collaborating on group projects, most software we use daily relies entirely on remote data centers. Three specific cloud applications I open almost every day are **Spotify** for audio streaming, **Netflix** for video entertainment, and **Facebook Messenger** for staying connected with peers and family.

## 2. Identifying Service and Deployment Models
These platforms are built on distinct architecture frameworks designed to balance user access with data protection:
- **Spotify** operates as **Software as a Service (SaaS)** and uses a **Public Cloud** deployment model. As an end-user, I interact purely with the application interface to stream music, while the underlying server resources, audio catalogs, and algorithmic systems run on standard, publicly shared cloud environments managed by the provider.
- **Netflix** is similarly categorized as a **SaaS** application deployed on a **Public Cloud**. The platform serves high-definition video content straight to consumer devices via a web browser or app. It utilizes highly scalable public cloud infrastructure to distribute heavy traffic and handle massive bandwidth demands globally.
- **Facebook Messenger** acts as a **SaaS** platform but follows a **Hybrid Cloud** deployment strategy. To guarantee instantaneous message routing and real-time connectivity for millions of global profiles, it relies on flexible public networks. However, the secure master directories, critical encryption systems, and core user records remain strictly within private, proprietary data centers to prevent data leaks.

## 3. Git & GitHub for Cloud Projects
Transitioning into developing cloud systems means dealing with an intricate web of environmental variables, codebases, and server configurations where a single typo can take down an entire live project. This risk makes version control frameworks like Git and GitHub absolutely indispensable for software engineers.

Git works as a continuous tracking ledger, establishing a precise baseline for every single update. If an unstable line of code breaks a production environment during deployment, developers can easily execute a roll-back to the last verified, functional commit. Furthermore, GitHub acts as the central collaboration hub for teams. It lets engineers run isolated tracks of development via feature branches, preventing conflicts and messy code overwrites. By utilizing pull requests and peer code reviews, development teams can audit configurations and spot security flaws before updates ever touch live cloud architecture, maintaining overall system reliability.
